\chapter{Graphics, UI, and device-facing kernels}
\label{ch:107}
\index{Graphics, UI, and device-facing kernels}

The graphics inventory includes representation and geometry utilities, software or device-backed rendering paths, and overlay/display components. Pure integer geometry, a CPU buffer and a display resource have different error and lifetime contracts. Device-facing profiles can depend on the selected platform and host permissions. A kernel header with framebuffer or display functionality does not provide a source On1x rendering API automatically.

\section{Separate deterministic and host tests}
Verify coordinate transforms and clipping against data fixtures, with cases on boundaries and outside the intended viewport. Test row layout in a CPU buffer independently of a device. For a platform adapter, record which target, device configuration and presentation path were actually used. Compare teardown and failure behavior before testing a long interactive scene. If exposing graphics to source, start with a bounded copy-safe operation or an existing native facade. Broader resource APIs require explicit lifetimes for frames, buffers, events and any foreign handles.

\section{Build a readback oracle in ordinary RAM}
Adapt the framebuffer example to a surface two pixels wide, two high,
with pitch three words and capacity five words. Initialize all five
words to a sentinel different from either test color. The visible
indices are zero, one, three and four. With tile size one, colors A
and B, the rows must be A B and B A. Index two remains the sentinel.
There need not be a sixth word: the contract requires the last visible
pixel to fit, not an extra padding word after it.

Read the buffer yourself before calling the pattern verifier. Otherwise
the writer and reader could both use the same wrong pitch and still
agree on zero mismatches. Change the word at index four from A to B;
verification should now report one mismatch. Restore it, then change
only the sentinel at index two; verification should again report zero.
Reducing the declared capacity to four makes the surface invalid. In
that case the verifier returns \OnxCodeTerm{SIZE\_MAX}, which should
not be presented as a measured count of bad pixels.

Why use raw color words in this exercise? Row addressing is independent
of which firmware masks encode red or blue. Test packing separately:
in RGB565, full red with green and blue zero should set the five red
bits and leave all other bits clear. Overlapping channel masks must fail
without changing the output word. Only after both tests agree is it
useful to ask whether a device mapping supports reliable readback.

Record pitch as three \emph{words} in this case. Recording twelve bytes
in the pitch field instead would put the predicted second row at word
twelve and invalidate the five-word surface before drawing begins.

